@database "media_ops"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: media_ops.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Media operations"
@{h1}Media operations

Moves images, sounds and videos between the disk and an agent's
conversation, in both directions. An agent loads a file that is already on
disk -- a render, a recording, a screenshot -- so its model can look at it
or listen to it. And an agent writes media that only exists in the
conversation -- an image you pasted into the chat -- to a file, so it
survives the run and other tools can use it. Every path, read or written,
must lie inside the folders the plugin is given.

The plugin has no panel and no hooks.

@{code}
For agents and developers
  @{"  Loading a file  " link load}
  @{"  Listing and saving the conversation's media  " link save}
  @{"  Where it may read and write  " link sandbox}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node load "Loading a file"
@{h1}Loading a file

@{tt}media_ops_load@{utt} (the instance name followed by @{tt}_load@{utt})
attaches one file to its answer, for the model to see or hear.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
path | the file; absolute, or relative to the folder the server was started in (the project); data/... names the data directory
@{body}

The file must lie inside @{tt}allowed_directories@{utt} (see
@{" Where it may read and write " link sandbox}), exist, carry one of these
extensions (upper or lower case) and not be too large:

@{table}
Kind | Extensions
image | .png .jpg .jpeg .gif .webp .bmp
audio | .wav .mp3 .ogg .flac .m4a .aac
video | .mp4 .webm .avi .mov
@{body}

The kind and the mime type come from the extension alone; the file's
content is not looked at, and nothing is decoded.

@{h2}The size limit

A file larger than @{tt}max_file_size_mb@{utt} (default 20) is refused
with its size. The limit can be raised only up to what a request attaches:
20 MB for an image or a video, 25 MB for a sound. A file above those would
be left out of every request without a word, so the plugin refuses it
instead, and the error names the limit that applies.

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
status | success
path | the resolved, absolute path
type | image, audio or video
mime_type | e.g. image/png
size_mb | the file's size, rounded to two places
@{body}

The file itself is attached beside the answer; how it reaches the model
is in @{" What the model sees " link model}. Errors are answers, not
exceptions: @{tt}status: error@{utt}, @{tt}error@{utt} and
@{tt}error_type@{utt}:

@{table}
error_type | When
ValidationError | path missing or not a string
PermissionError | the path lies outside the folders, or cannot be resolved
FileNotFoundError | no file there (also for a folder)
UnsupportedMediaType | an extension not in the table; the error lists the ones that work
FileTooLarge | above the limit; the error names size and limit
@{body}

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"path": "data/comfyui/outputs/comfy_00012_.png"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node save "Listing and saving the conversation's media"
@{h1}Listing and saving the conversation's media

Media that arrived in the conversation inline -- an image pasted into the
chat, a picture a tool returned as data -- exists only there. These two
tools put it on disk.

@{h2}media_ops_list_context

Takes no parameters. Reads the calling agent's current conversation (the
live messages, else the stored session) and lists every image, sound and
video in it: the items of a message's content and the files tools
attached. Remote URLs and text are skipped. An item that occurs twice
(same bytes, or same path) is listed once, at its first place.

@{table}
Field | Meaning
id | a handle for save: m and 12 hex digits from the bytes, or from the path of a file; the same item keeps its id between calls
type | image, audio or video
message_index | the message it sits in
origin | content (the message itself) or multimodal_content (a tool's attachment)
inline | true: the bytes are in the conversation; false: a file on disk
mime_type | as the item states it
size_bytes | the payload's size; for a file its size on disk, empty when it is gone
path | only for a file
name | only inline, when the item carries a file name
error | only for an item whose base64 cannot be decoded; its id is then empty
@{body}

The answer is @{tt}status: success@{utt}, @{tt}count@{utt} and
@{tt}media@{utt}. Called outside a session (no agent or no session id) it
answers @{tt}error_type: SessionContextMissing@{utt} rather than an empty
list, so "nothing there" and "could not look" do not read alike.

@{h2}media_ops_save

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
id | an id from media_ops_list_context
path | the target file, with a supported extension; absolute or relative as for load
overwrite | true replaces an existing file; otherwise it is refused
@{body}

What happens, in this order:

@{bullet}A read-only instance refuses at once (@{tt}PermissionError@{utt}),
whatever the arguments.
@{bullet}Missing id or path: @{tt}ValidationError@{utt}. No session:
@{tt}SessionContextMissing@{utt}.
@{bullet}The conversation is read again and the id looked up: unknown is
@{tt}MediaNotFound@{utt} (the error says how many items there are), an
undecodable item @{tt}MediaUnreadable@{utt}.
@{bullet}An item that is already a file is not copied: the answer is
@{tt}copied: false@{utt} with its existing @{tt}path@{utt}, and the
@{tt}path@{utt} argument is not used at all.
@{bullet}Otherwise the target must lie inside the folders
(@{tt}PermissionError@{utt}), carry an extension from the load table
(@{tt}UnsupportedMediaType@{utt}) and not exist unless
@{tt}overwrite@{utt} is true (@{tt}FileExists@{utt}).
@{bullet}Missing folders are created and the bytes written as they are.
The extension is not checked against the content: an image saved as
@{tt}.mp3@{utt} is written.

The answer: @{tt}status: success@{utt}, @{tt}path@{utt},
@{tt}copied: true@{utt}, @{tt}bytes@{utt} and @{tt}type@{utt}. A write the
operating system refuses (a folder at the target, no permission) ends the
call with that error.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{}
{"id": "m3f2a9c01b7de", "path": "data/workspace/pasted_diagram.png"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node sandbox "Where it may read and write"
@{h1}Where it may read and write

The paths come from the model, so without a boundary @{tt}load@{utt} would
read any file into the conversation and @{tt}save@{utt} write anywhere.
Both go through the core's path sandbox, the same check
@{" file_ops " link file_ops/sandbox} uses:

@{bullet}The path is resolved first -- @{tt}..@{utt} collapsed, links and
junctions followed -- and must then lie inside one of
@{tt}allowed_directories@{utt}. A link inside that points outside is
refused.
@{bullet}A network or device path (@{tt}\\\\host\\share\\x.png@{utt},
@{tt}//host/share/x.png@{utt}, @{tt}\\\\?\\UNC\\...@{utt},
@{tt}\\??\\...@{utt}) is refused on its text alone, before anything is
opened -- resolving it would make Windows sign in to that host with your
credentials.
@{bullet}A path starting with @{tt}~@{utt} is refused: it is not your home
folder here.
@{bullet}A relative path counts from the folder the server started in; a
@{tt}data/...@{utt} path, and a @{tt}data/...@{utt} entry in the list,
follow the data directory when it is moved.
@{bullet}Without @{tt}allowed_directories@{utt} the instance gets the data
directory. An empty list allows nothing.
@{bullet}@{tt}read_only: true@{utt} keeps loading and listing, but
@{tt}save@{utt} is not offered to the model and refuses if called anyway.

Each folder in the list can be read into the model's context and written
by every agent that has the tools. Keep it small.

@{tt}list_context@{utt} reads file paths out of the conversation, which
tools put there. It never looks at a network or device path on disk; such
an item is listed with an empty size.

@{h2}Undo

The file checkpoints plugin records a @{tt}save@{utt} before it runs --
the target and any folder it creates, resolved by this plugin's own
sandbox -- so a rewind restores what was there
(@{" What is recorded " link file_checkpoints/recorded}). A save the
sandbox refuses is not recorded.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The descriptions come from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. A
read-only instance offers only @{tt}load@{utt} and
@{tt}list_context@{utt}. Refusals the model gets verbatim include:

@{code}
Path is outside the allowed directories: <path>. Allowed: <folders>
File is too large for the context: 31.4 MB (limit 20 MB): <path>
Unsupported file type: .txt. Supported extensions: .aac, .avi, ...
No media with id 'm...' in the current context. Call media_ops_list_context for the current ids (3 item(s) available).
File already exists: <path>. Pass overwrite=true to replace it.
Sandbox is read-only, refusing to write: <path>
@{body}

The chat's status line ends with "Loaded shot.png [image, 1.2 MB]",
"3 media item(s) in context", "Saved image -> diagram.png (84.0 KB)" or
"Already on disk: <path> -- nothing written"; a refusal shows as an error.

@{h2}The attached file

The conversation keeps only the file's path. Each request to the model
reads the file again and sends it along, base64-encoded, after the tool
answer -- so a file changed on disk later is seen as it is then, and a
deleted one drops out. What arrives depends on the model and its
provider:

@{bullet}A model without image input gets a note naming the file, not the
file.
@{bullet}Images reach every provider with image input.
@{bullet}Sound reaches a model that takes audio input; otherwise a note.
.flac, .ogg and the other formats besides .wav and .mp3 are converted to
MP3 first when pydub (with ffmpeg) is installed.
@{bullet}Video arrives as a video only where the provider takes inline
video (Gemini's own client); elsewhere as a note.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are appended; nothing earlier changes. The JSON of a load costs
tens of tokens; the attachment costs what the provider charges for it --
the core's context estimate counts 1,120 tokens per image -- on every later
request of the run,
since it stays in the conversation. Sound and video cost by length. The
attachment is built the same way each time, so an unchanged file does not
break the prompt cache. @{tt}list_context@{utt} sends no media, only the
list.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}The size limit is per file; there is no limit on how many files
are loaded or saved, nor on the pixel count of an image. Its dimensions
are the provider's business.
@{bullet}@{tt}save@{utt} writes the bytes of an item; it cannot convert
between formats.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    media_ops:
      type: media_ops
      enabled: true
      description: "Load media files from disk into the agent's context, and save context media back to disk"
      allowed_directories:
        - data
      max_file_size_mb: 20
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+media_ops/*"
@{body}

The plugin needs nothing beyond the core. Converting sound for some
providers uses pydub and ffmpeg when they are there.

@{h2}Server settings

The settings may stand in the entry itself or under @{tt}config:@{utt};
the latter wins. They are read when the server starts.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
allowed_directories | the data directory | the folders load reads and save writes; an empty list allows nothing
max_file_size_mb | 20 | the largest file load takes, at most 20 for images and videos and 25 for sound
read_only | false | true: save is not offered and refuses
@{body}

The plugin keeps no data of its own; it writes only the files
@{tt}save@{utt} is told to write.
@endnode
